1 SPAWN

   Creates a subprocess of the current process, as DCL's SPAWN and a
   program's LIB$SPAWN do, and waits for it to end unless /NOWAIT.

   Format

     SPAWN [command]
     SPAWN/HOST command

2 Parameter

 command

   A command for the subprocess to run before it logs out.  Without
   one, the subprocess reads commands from its input until the end of
   the input, LOGOUT, or EXIT.  With /HOST, a program on the host and
   its arguments, which /HOST requires.

2 Description

   The subprocess runs DCL, the same command interpreter as the
   console, with its own symbols, logical names, default directory,
   $STATUS, and open files.  Every DCL command works there, command
   procedures (@) included.  The commands that work the machine itself
   (DEBUG, ASM, INITIALIZE/VAX, INITIALIZE/MEMORY, SAVE, LOAD, ...) are
   the console's own, and give %VAX-E-NOTSUBPROC.  A subprocess can SPAWN a
   subprocess of its own; a process's subprocesses end when it does.

   SPAWN says when the subprocess has been made (%DCL-S-SPAWNED, and
   %DCL-S-ATTACHED if it waits) and when control is back
   (%DCL-S-RETURNED).  Its status is the subprocess's final status.  A
   SPAWN that waits gives the subprocess the terminal; ATTACH gives it
   back, or to another process of the job.

   SPAWN needs vax.process.scheduler, which is on unless the
   configuration turns it off (see CONFIGURATION KEYS).

2 Qualifiers
/HOST

      /HOST

   Runs the command as a program on the host, with exec, instead of
   making a subprocess; govax's own.  The command is split into the
   program and its arguments at blanks, and single or double quotes
   keep a word together; put a word that starts with "/" in quotes, or
   DCL takes it for a qualifier.  No shell is involved, so name one
   for a shell's pipes and wildcards.  DCL's 'symbol' substitution
   happens first, as on any command line.

   SPAWN waits, the machine standing still, and the program has the
   terminal.  With /NOWAIT it runs beside the console with no input but
   /INPUT.  DCL shows what it writes, unless /OUTPUT, between commands
   and at the prompt.  A /NOWAIT program still running when govax ends
   ends with it.

   /INPUT and /OUTPUT are its standard input and output: a host file
   or a volume file, by the usual rules for a name.  /SYMBOLS and
   /LOGICAL_NAMES (or /LOGICALS) add to its environment, only if given:
   GOVAX_SYM_name for each DCL symbol and GOVAX_LOG_name for each
   process logical name, with a "$" in the name changed to "_".  So
   PARTS$DATA is GOVAX_LOG_PARTS_DATA, and a search list's values are
   separated by commas.  /PROCESS and /PROMPT can't be used with it.

   A program that can't run or ends with a failure gives %VAX-E-HOSTCMD.

/INPUT

      /INPUT=filespec

   The subprocess's SYS$INPUT: the terminal, a mailbox, or a file of
   commands.  The default is the current process's.

/LOGICAL_NAMES

      /[NO]LOGICAL_NAMES

   Copies the current process's process logical names to the
   subprocess (the default, /LOGICAL_NAMES).

/LOGICALS

      /[NO]LOGICALS

   The same as /LOGICAL_NAMES.

/OUTPUT

      /OUTPUT=filespec

   The subprocess's SYS$OUTPUT: a terminal, a mailbox, NL:, or a file,
   which gets each line as a record.

/PROCESS

      /PROCESS=name

   The subprocess's name.  The default is the user name and "_n".

/PROMPT

      /PROMPT=string

   The prompt for commands read from the terminal.  The default is
   "$ ".

/SYMBOLS

      /[NO]SYMBOLS

   Copies the current process's symbols to the subprocess (the default,
   /SYMBOLS).

/WAIT

      /[NO]WAIT

   /NOWAIT doesn't wait: the subprocess runs beside the console.  It
   takes turns with the console's DCL: between its commands (one command
   each, in a procedure), during WAIT, while the machine runs (RUN, GO,
   CALL), and while the VAX> prompt waits for a line.  What it writes
   meanwhile appears above the line being typed, which is shown again
   below it.  (With input from a pipe, not a terminal, it runs at the
   prompt only until it waits or has run a while.)

2 Examples

   1.$ SPAWN DIRECTORY

     Lists the default directory in a subprocess, and waits for it.

   2.$ SPAWN

     Starts a subprocess reading commands from the terminal, until it
     logs out or ATTACHes back.

   3.$ SPAWN/NOWAIT/OUTPUT=NIGHTLY.LOG @NIGHTLY

     Runs NIGHTLY.COM in a subprocess beside the console.

   4.$ SPAWN/HOST/SYMBOLS/OUTPUT=LIST.TXT sh -c "ls *.go; env"

     Runs a shell on the host, with DCL's symbols in its environment,
     and makes LIST.TXT of what it writes.
